<p>Let <m>R</m> be the
rectangle  <m>3 \leq x \leq 3.6</m>,
<m>5 \leq y \leq 6</m>.  Find the values of Riemann
sums which are reasonable over- and under-estimates for
<m>\int_R f(x,y) \,dA</m> with <m>\Delta x=0.3</m> and <m>\Delta y=0.5</m>.</p>
<p>over-estimate: <fillin characters="25" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p>under-estimate: <fillin characters="25" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>35\cdot 0.3\cdot 0.5</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>20\cdot 0.3\cdot 0.5</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><image xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org/2020/pretext/internal" pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__Michigan__Chap16Sec1__Q01__pg-image-1.png" width="41%"/>




Then we can calculate the lower and upper sum by taking the smallest
and largest values on each rectangle:
<me>\mbox{Riemann sum} = \sum f(x_i,y_i)\Delta x\Delta y,</me>
so the lower sum is
<me>\mbox{Lower} = (5 + 6 + 4 + 5)(0.3)(0.5) = (20)(0.3)(0.5) = 3,</me>
and the upper sum is
<me>\mbox{Upper} = (7 + 9 + 6 + 13)(0.3)(0.5) = (35)(0.3)(0.5) = 5.25.</me>



</solution>
